::CW_U05_L01_Q01::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Draft excerpt: The poem says I miss the park. Feedback: Add a sensory detail from the park. Which revision best uses the feedback?{
~A. I miss the park a lot.#Incorrect. This repeats the feeling without sensory detail.
~B. Parks are outside.#Incorrect. This is general and not a revision.
~C. The poem is now finished.#Incorrect. This does not revise the draft. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. The wet swings smelled like metal after the rain.#Correct. This adds a sensory detail from the park, which directly uses the feedback.
}

::CW_U05_L01_Q02::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Feedback: Your ending is confusing because I cannot tell why the character leaves. What should the revision goal be?{
~A. Change every character name.#Incorrect. Names are not the issue. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. Clarify the character's reason for leaving.#Correct. The feedback identifies unclear motivation, so the revision goal should address why the character leaves.
~C. Remove the ending completely without replacing it.#Incorrect. That may create a bigger gap. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Add unrelated description of weather.#Incorrect. Weather does not solve motivation unless connected.
}

::CW_U05_L01_Q03::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Online feedback flags a sentence as long and confusing. What should the writer do first?{
~A. Accept the tool's replacement automatically.#Incorrect. The replacement may damage voice. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Ignore the feedback because it came from a tool.#Incorrect. Tool feedback can identify real clarity problems.
=C. Review the sentence and decide whether shortening or dividing it would improve clarity while preserving voice.#Correct. The writer should evaluate the tool's concern before accepting or rejecting the suggestion.
~D. Delete all long sentences in the draft.#Incorrect. Some long sentences are purposeful. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L01_Q04::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Peer feedback: I like it. What is the main problem with this feedback?{
=A. It is too general to guide revision.#Correct. "I like it" does not name a craft strength, concern, or next step.
~B. It is too specific.#Incorrect. It is not specific. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. It gives too much evidence.#Incorrect. It gives no evidence. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. It is a convention correction.#Incorrect. It does not address conventions. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L01_Q05::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Draft excerpt: The room was scary. Feedback: Show the fear through setting details. Which revision best responds?{
~A. The room was very scary and really frightening.#Incorrect. This repeats the feeling without showing it.
=B. The ceiling bulb flickered, and every shadow leaned toward the door.#Correct. This shows fear through setting details instead of simply naming the emotion.
~C. I decided to write about a beach instead.#Incorrect. That avoids the feedback. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. The room had walls.#Incorrect. This detail is too basic to create fear.
}

::CW_U05_L01_Q06::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Adult feedback and peer feedback conflict. Adult says the ending needs more clarity; peer says the mystery is interesting. What should the writer do?{
~A. Ignore both comments.#Incorrect. Both may contain useful information. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Remove the ending.#Incorrect. That does not solve the conflict. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Add a random explanation unrelated to the story.#Incorrect. Revision should be purposeful. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. Identify what clarity is needed while preserving the useful mystery.#Correct. The writer should evaluate both comments and revise in a targeted way.
}

::CW_U05_L01_Q07::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Feedback: The dialogue sounds the same for both characters. Which revision goal is best?{
~A. Remove all dialogue.#Incorrect. The issue is not that dialogue exists.
~B. Add more setting description only.#Incorrect. Setting does not solve similar voices. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. Give each character a distinct voice through word choice, sentence length, or speech pattern.#Correct. This directly addresses the feedback about similar dialogue.
~D. Change the title.#Incorrect. The title is not the issue. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L01_Q08::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Draft excerpt: I was sad. Feedback: Use an action to show sadness. Which revision best uses the feedback?{
=A. I folded the letter twice, then slipped it into the drawer without reading it again.#Correct. This uses action to imply sadness. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~B. I was sad and sadder.#Incorrect. This repeats the emotion. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Sad means unhappy.#Incorrect. This defines rather than revises. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. The story has a narrator.#Incorrect. This does not show sadness. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L01_Q09::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Which feedback is most actionable?{
~A. This is good.#Incorrect. It is too general. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. The ending feels sudden because the character leaves before readers understand her choice.#Correct. This feedback names a specific problem and gives revision direction.
~C. I do not like it.#Incorrect. It gives no revision direction. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Make it different.#Incorrect. This is vague. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L01_Q10::[MLA.CW.REV.01] A writer receives online feedback to replace a poetic fragment with a complete sentence. The fragment is intentional and creates emphasis. What should the writer do?{
~A. Always remove every fragment.#Incorrect. Some fragments are purposeful in creative writing.
~B. Accept every grammar suggestion without thinking.#Incorrect. Tools do not always understand creative effect.
=C. Keep the fragment if it serves the poem's emphasis and does not create accidental confusion.#Correct. Creative choices should be evaluated for purpose and reader effect.
~D. Delete the poem.#Incorrect. That is unnecessary. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L01_Q11::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Draft excerpt: The character is brave. Feedback: Show bravery through a choice. Which revision best responds?{
~A. She was brave, courageous, and brave.#Incorrect. This tells and repeats. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Bravery is important.#Incorrect. This is general. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. The dog was brown.#Incorrect. Color alone does not show bravery. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. She stepped between her brother and the broken window, even before she knew what to say.#Correct. This shows bravery through a concrete choice.
}

::CW_U05_L01_Q12::[MLA.CW.REV.01] What is the best reason to set aside feedback?{
~A. The feedback is specific and useful.#Incorrect. Specific useful feedback should usually be considered.
=B. The feedback conflicts with the writer's intended purpose and would weaken the piece.#Correct. Feedback can be set aside when it does not support the writer's goal.
~C. The feedback identifies a real clarity problem.#Incorrect. Real clarity problems should be addressed. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. The feedback comes from a reader.#Incorrect. Reader feedback can be valuable. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L01_Q13::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Feedback: The poem's strongest image is the cracked cup. Which revision best builds on that feedback?{
=A. Repeat the cracked cup image at the ending to connect the poem's beginning and close.#Correct. This builds on the strongest image identified by feedback.
~B. Remove the cracked cup.#Incorrect. The feedback identifies it as strong. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Add a spaceship.#Incorrect. This is unrelated. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Change the poem to an essay about cups.#Incorrect. That changes the genre unnecessarily. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L01_Q14::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Why should a writer explain a revision after using feedback?{
~A. To prove all feedback is wrong.#Incorrect. The goal is not to reject feedback.
~B. To avoid revising.#Incorrect. Explanation follows revision. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. To show how the feedback led to a purposeful craft decision.#Correct. Explaining revision helps connect feedback, change, and reader effect.
~D. To fill space only.#Incorrect. It has a craft purpose. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L01_Q15::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Peer feedback: I was confused when the speaker switched from childhood to the present. What should the writer revise?{
~A. Change every image to a color.#Incorrect. That does not solve time confusion. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Remove all memories.#Incorrect. Memories may be central. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Add a new character with no role.#Incorrect. This adds confusion. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. Add a clear transition that signals the move from childhood memory to present time.#Correct. The feedback identifies a confusing time shift, so a transition is needed.
}

::CW_U05_L01_Q16::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Draft excerpt: The scene has no clear conflict. Feedback: Give the character something difficult to choose. Which revision best responds?{
~A. Add a list of furniture.#Incorrect. Furniture does not create conflict by itself.
=B. Give the character two choices that both have consequences.#Correct. A difficult choice creates conflict. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~C. Change all punctuation.#Incorrect. Punctuation does not add conflict. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Remove the character's goal.#Incorrect. That weakens conflict. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L01_Q17::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Which is the best revision plan format?{
=A. Feedback: ending is sudden; Goal: clarify decision; Revision: add one action showing why the character leaves.#Correct. This plan connects feedback, goal, and revision.
~B. Feedback: none; Goal: none; Revision: random changes.#Incorrect. This is not a plan. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Feedback: good; Goal: good; Revision: good.#Incorrect. This is vague. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Feedback: change everything; Goal: delete all voice.#Incorrect. This is not targeted. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L01_Q18::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Feedback: The speaker's voice is strong, but one image is confusing. What is the best revision approach?{
~A. Rewrite the whole piece in a formal academic voice.#Incorrect. That may erase the strong voice. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Ignore the confusing image.#Incorrect. The feedback identifies a real issue. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. Revise only the confusing image while preserving the strong speaker voice.#Correct. The feedback says the voice is strong, so the revision should be targeted.
~D. Remove all images.#Incorrect. Only one image is the problem. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L01_Q19::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Draft excerpt: The poem repeats the word cold six times. Feedback: The repetition works at first but becomes distracting. What should the writer do?{
~A. Remove every repeated word automatically.#Incorrect. Some repetition can be purposeful. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Keep all repetitions because feedback never matters.#Incorrect. The reader identified a pattern problem. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Add more cold repetitions.#Incorrect. That likely increases the problem. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. Keep the strongest repetitions and remove or replace the ones that distract.#Correct. This preserves purposeful repetition while responding to feedback.
}

::CW_U05_L01_Q20::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Which feedback best supports revision for structure?{
~A. The word blue appears twice.#Incorrect. That is word-level, not structure. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. The middle scene arrives before the reader understands why the character left home.#Correct. This feedback addresses sequence and organization. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~C. I like the main character.#Incorrect. This is not structural feedback. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Use a comma in sentence two.#Incorrect. That is convention feedback. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L01_Q21::[MLA.CW.REV.01] A writer receives feedback from three sources. What should the writer do first?{
~A. Accept the longest comment automatically.#Incorrect. Length does not equal usefulness. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Reject all comments.#Incorrect. Some may be useful. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. Read all comments and group them by purpose, clarity, structure, style, or conventions.#Correct. Sorting feedback helps the writer plan targeted revision.
~D. Revise without reading the comments.#Incorrect. Feedback must be considered. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L01_Q22::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Which revision best protects writer purpose? Purpose: create quiet grief. Feedback: Add more emotion.{
=A. Add one restrained image, such as an untouched coat by the door, to deepen the grief quietly.#Correct. This adds emotion while protecting the quiet purpose.
~B. Add five exclamation points.#Incorrect. That may break the quiet mood. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Turn the piece into a comedy.#Incorrect. That changes the purpose. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Remove the grief entirely.#Incorrect. That removes the purpose. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L01_Q23::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Which online feedback should be treated with caution in creative writing?{
~A. A spelling correction for an accidental typo.#Incorrect. This is usually useful. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. A warning about a missing period in standard prose.#Incorrect. This may be useful. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. A note that a word may be misspelled.#Incorrect. This should be checked. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. A grammar suggestion that removes an intentional fragment used for emphasis.#Correct. Online tools may misread purposeful creative choices as errors.
}

::CW_U05_L01_Q24::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Feedback: The reader wants to know what the character wants. Which revision best responds?{
=A. Add a moment that shows the character's goal or desire.#Correct. The revision directly answers what the reader needs to know.
~B. Add a random weather detail.#Incorrect. Weather does not explain the goal. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Remove the character from the story.#Incorrect. That avoids the problem. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Change the font.#Incorrect. Formatting does not clarify desire. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L01_Q25::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Which sentence best explains a revision?{
~A. I changed stuff because it was bad.#Incorrect. This is vague. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. I revised the final image so the reader could infer the speaker's grief instead of being told directly.#Correct. This explains the specific change and its reader effect.
~C. The draft is better now.#Incorrect. This does not explain how. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Someone told me to revise.#Incorrect. This does not explain the revision. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}
